finding: P1: src/standard/rendering/pipelines.ts:96-109,1286-1295,1336 replaces hardware AlphaToCoverage unconditionally, including lone/bottom cameras, with a fixed first-N-samples mask. This repairs stored alpha but changes single-camera RGB coverage and removes hardware spatial dithering; the stack needs coverage-normalized alpha only for upper images, not a global replacement of bottom-camera coverage. Independently compared the old hardware and new exact mask algorithm on a default-limit WebGPU device using a 64x64 horizontal alpha gradient: 1024 pixels changed RGB; at x=2,y=0 hardware resolved red=64 while fixed mask resolved red=0, and x=18,y=0 hardware red=128 while fixed red=64. The new fog test at src/extras/fog/stacked.node.ts:63-90 asserts scene alpha is 1, not unchanged bottom RGB, so it passes despite this regression. Keep the ordinary bottom/lone hardware path (and its original fog behavior, or preserve its opaque alpha separately) and restrict any necessary coverage normalization to upper images; lazy stack-only variants preserve single-camera pipeline/allocation counts. Existing prepass/shadow cutoff paths remain unchanged and are not sample-mask-consistent, which is an existing limitation rather than evidence this universal replacement preserves frames.
